{# Rendered into k8s/roles/pihole's ConfigMap on daniel-box, its only consumer. #}
{# The wildcard answers the cluster ingress VIP on every Pi-hole. There is no
   default(server_ip) fallback on purpose: it would make the answer depend on which host
   rendered the file. #}
{# DECIDED (2026-08-24): a matching PUBLIC wildcard `*.local.<domain>` -> this same VIP is
   authoritative on Cloudflare, and it STAYS. It is not managed by this repo — cloudflare-ddns
   handles only the apex, terraria and valheim — so it reads as a stray record leaking the
   internal ingress VIP to anyone who asks 1.1.1.1, including for names that do not exist.
   Removing it was proposed and is WRONG, because two paths depend on it:

     1. CoreDNS forwards `policy sequential` with health_check/max_fails 2 (see
        k8s/roles/coredns/templates/config/coredns-corefile.j2), Pi-hole first and the public resolvers
        after. During a Pi-hole outage — the exact case that failover exists for — in-cluster
        `.local.` lookups reach 1.1.1.1 and still land on the right VIP BECAUSE of the public
        record. Drop it and that degradation becomes NXDOMAIN.
     2. daniel-box's own host resolver has the same failover. Its /etc/resolv.conf names
        only 127.0.0.1 (hosts/components/pi-resolver/templates/resolv.conf.j2, which
        hosts/components/k3s-host-dns/ renders), and the host CoreDNS forwarder behind it
        (hosts/components/k3s-host-dns/templates/host-corefile.j2) forwards `policy
        sequential` with health_check, Pi-hole's VIP first and 1.1.1.1 and 1.0.0.1 after. It
        serves no `.local.` zone of its own, so during a Pi-hole outage host-level `.local.`
        lookups reach 1.1.1.1 too. Drop the record and those lookups on the control-plane
        node go NXDOMAIN for exactly as long as Pi-hole is down.

   The exposure is reconnaissance only: 10.0.0.240 is unroutable from the internet, so the
   record discloses an RFC1918 address and the existence of the naming scheme, nothing more.
   ACME is unaffected either way — issuance uses the DNS-01 TXT challenge, not this A record. #}
address=/local.{{ domain }}/{{ pihole_wildcard_ip | default(k3s_metallb_ingress_vip) }}
{# DECIDED: `local=` here, not `address=/local.<domain>/::`. Both keep an
   AAAA for a `.local.` name from being forwarded upstream, which is the property that
   matters — since dnsmasq 2.86 an `address=` carrying only an IPv4 sends every OTHER record
   type upstream, past the public resolvers this file exists to keep LAN names away from.
   The `::` form answered that AAAA with a NULL address, which is a
   routable-looking answer to a client that prefers IPv6: Happy Eyeballs clients stall on it
   briefly, and grpc-go retried `[::]:443` forever (2026-08-10, the otel-collector forwarder).
   `local=` answers it NODATA instead, the shape dnsmasq's own manual prescribes:
   "--address=/example.com/1.2.3.4 --local=/example.com/" restores the pre-2.86 NoData. #}
local=/local.{{ domain }}/

# `extra_hostnames` covers an entry fronting several routed workloads; otherwise a second name falls
# through to the local.<domain> wildcard and the Docker Traefik answers 404.
{% for name in services | lan_hostnames %}
address=/{{ name }}.local.{{ domain }}/{{ k3s_metallb_ingress_vip }}
{% endfor %}

# Private `.lan` TLD, not a <domain> subdomain: HSTS with stsIncludeSubdomains force-upgrades
# *.<domain> to HTTPS and breaks the Pi's plain-HTTP services.
host-record=daniel-pi.lan,{{ hosts['daniel-pi'].server_ip }}

# The BARE names are load-bearing: otel-sweep addresses `daniel-box`/`daniel-server` from a fixed
# enum and the client SSH alias is not deployed to these hosts. /etc/hosts still resolves a node's
# own name.
host-record=daniel-box.lan,daniel-box,{{ hosts['daniel-box'].server_ip }}
host-record=daniel-server.lan,daniel-server,{{ hosts['daniel-server'].server_ip }}

# Conditional forwarding for LAN reverse-DNS. Without it Pi-hole answers PTR only for the
# `host-record=` names above, so every other LAN device — phones, laptops, anything holding a
# DHCP lease — appears in the query log and on the dashboard as a bare IP. The router already
# knows those names from the leases it handed out; this forwards the LAN's reverse zone to it.
#
# `server=` rather than Pi-hole's own `rev-server=`, for two reasons. Plain dnsmasq syntax
# cannot be a directive FTL's config parser rejects at startup, and this file is read by BOTH
# Pi-hole instances — a rejected directive is a fleet-wide DNS outage with no working resolver
# left to fetch the fix through. `rev-server=<cidr>,<server>,<domain>` also expands to a second
# `server=/<domain>/<router>` forward, which would send the `.lan` names the host-records above
# answer for to the router instead. The narrow form forwards reverse queries and nothing else.
#
# Local records still win: dnsmasq answers a PTR it knows from `host-record=` itself and
# forwards only what it does not know, so the node names above keep their `.lan` answers.
#
# The zone is derived from `lan_subnet` rather than typed, so a subnet change moves both
# together. Only whole octets are used — correct for the /24 this LAN is, and a prefix that is
# not a multiple of 8 would need RFC 2317 delegation, which this router does not do.
{% set _lan_octets = lan_subnet.split('/')[0].split('.') %}
{% set _lan_prefix = lan_subnet.split('/')[1] | int %}
server=/{{ _lan_octets[:(_lan_prefix // 8)] | reverse | join('.') }}.in-addr.arpa/{{ lan_router_ip }}
